\title{Contractibility Is Equivalent to Singleton Induction}
\taxon{Theorem}
\import{macros}
\p{
    A type #{A} is contractible if and only if it has a point #{a:A} at which
    it satisfies [singleton induction](def-005J).
}
\proof{
    Suppose first that #{A} has center #{a:A} and contraction
    #{C:\Pi_{x:A}(a=x)}. Normalize the contraction by defining
    ##{
        C'(x)\defeq C(a)^{-1}\cdot C(x).
    }
    The left inverse law gives #{q:C'(a)=\refl_a}. For a family
    #{B:A\to\mathcal{U}} and #{b:B(a)}, define
    ##{
        \mathsf{indSing}_a(b,x)
        \defeq
        \mathsf{transport}_B(C'(x),b):B(x).
    }
    At #{x=a}, applying transport to #{q:C'(a)=\refl_a}, followed by the
    computation rule for transport along reflexivity, gives
    ##{
        \mathsf{indSing}_a(b,a)
        =\mathsf{transport}_B(\refl_a,b)
        =b.
    }
    This supplies the required section of evaluation.

    Conversely, suppose #{A} has a point #{a} and satisfies singleton
    induction. Apply it to the family #{B(x)\defeq(a=x)} and the element
    #{\refl_a:B(a)}. The resulting section is
    ##{
        \mathsf{indSing}_a(\refl_a):\Pi_{x:A}(a=x),
    }
    which is a contraction of #{A} with center #{a}.
}
